I love the Internet, Usenet, and all the on-line services which provide
me with a stream of never ending information. I like its anarchic
construction and immediate, direct access to information to and for all
(who can afford it). My concern is about the future of on-line information.
I am not worried about censors, or exorbitant hookup fees, what bothers me
most is having to pay for information? Nowadays, people only pay for 
information because a) it is convenient to do so, b) it is very new,
proprietary, or secret, or c) it is rare. One popular source of free 
information that may be in jeopardy is the public library system. Will
the future of libraries be resovoirs of pre-digital books, and magazines,
with limited "free" access to certain types of digital information? 
The Library of Congress and 
Ministries of Culture do have routine postings of rare information, 
such as The Dead Sea Scrolls. But, what would happen if all users chose
to access subscription newspapers, and magazines through these libraries?
Or choose to download books and print them out at home? The profitability
of many content providers will disappear as people realize they can just
download anything form their omniscent content provider, The Free Library. 
Will libraries threaten the status quo, will they be illegal?
And why shouldn't the library pay for all this on-line information, and
allow the public to access them for free? That is their traditional
role, after all. And if this is so, why can't the library be a free
on-line service provider as well. Do you pay the librarian everytime 
you ask for his/her help? Our taxes go to support the library to provide
us with all of these services before such a thing as the Web existed,
so I don't see any reason why it should stop now. 
 
  

