In article <800027178snz@microvst.demon.co.uk>
           tgold@microvst.demon.co.uk "Anthony R. Gold" writes:

> If I understand your position correctly, you propose that the law of the
> land, taken together with its history, provenance, etymology and whatnot
> comprises a constitution.  And I disagree.  I do not maintain that a
> constitution should be similar to any other or contain any particular
> rights, however I do believe that it should be more difficult and slower
> to change than the law of the land.  To me, the fact than in the first
> quarter of this century the emancipation of women was a simple act in
> Britain and a major effort in the USA clearly illustrates this difference. 

Then you appear to be redefining what a constitution is.

It is the rules that decide how laws are made, whether they have to be
obeyed, and what penalties there may be for disobedience.  At its simplest
it's "You'll do what *I* say", or it may be a complicated document.

Constitutions *may* include the rules for their change, and they *may* use
those rules when they're changed (but not always).  The simplest change
procedure is "<bang><bang>  Now you'll do what I say".

When they're changed they may be amended like the US or replaced like
France.

The fact that the procedures for changing in the US and the UK were
different has no more significance than that the US and the UK are
different.  I understand that Parliamentary Bills may be declared
"constitutional" by the Speaker, and the procedure for enacting them is
somewhat changed, though I'm not sure of the details.

You may consider my simple examples are somewhat facile, but I would point
out they're drawn from the real world.

