BOOKS                                                            BOOKS

                           THE NEW VICTORIANS
          A Young Woman's Challenge To The Old Feminist Order
                            By Rene Denfeld
                Warner Books/H.B. Fenn, $26.95 cloth

                                  by
                              MALENE ARPE

Let's summarize: women are delicate and therefore must be protected
from smut; women are inherently passive; a real lady is not interested
in sex; and ... all men are potential rapists.

No, this is not a lesson in 1890s morality, but, according to Rene
Denfeld, author of The New Victorians, a pretty accurate picture of
1990s feminist ideology.

The 27-year-old Portland native thinks that the leaders of the current
feminist movement have little to offer women of her own generation. "I
started writing on this topic because I was working as a bartender at
the time, and I was concerned that the women I was working with --
predominantly younger women, who were for equality, who cared about
sexism -- held these negative perceptions of feminism that are so
common today," Denfeld says in an interview.

In her book, Denfeld takes women of feminism's so-called second wave to
task for abandoning the ideals of equality and opportunity to become
victim- worshipping, castration-happy and censorship-obsessed. She says
that the preoccupation with a vast, vile patriarchy has "cast all women
as helpless puppets, instead of marshalling the powers we do have."

As personified by anti-pornographers Catharine MacKinnon and Andrea
Dworkin, what once was "one of the greatest movements of our times in
terms of the vast upheaval and change it caused" has become obsessed
with curtailing freedoms. Instead of dealing with inequality, abortion
rights, affordable day care and sexual violence, these crusaders expend
energy censoring centrefolds, finding the goddess within and vilifying
heterosexual sex. For the average young woman struggling to make ends
meet, these goals are a waste of time.

Denfield sprinkles her book with eerily familiar snippets of Victorian
writing, to drive home the similarity between two seemingly different
eras. "The message that women receive from feminist leaders," she
writes, "and, by default, the mainstream media, is that feminism is an
all-or-nothing proposition. Either one accepts New Victorianism without
question or one eschews the feminist title."

But it doesn't have to be that way, she argues. We should be allowed to
be feminist without living in celibacy and converting to ur-goddess
worship.

"It's a very big mistake to say that we need to create a separate
women's movement, because I do think that the feminist movement is very
intrinsically tied to the notion of equality. And there is no reason
women should leave feminism to these extremists who have hijacked it,"
she said. "We need to reclaim the feminist label and become active on
bread-and-butter issues, while remaining respectful of women's personal
choices."


