Like Rene Denfeld in The New Victorians, Nadine Strossen is afraid of
what a return to old-fashioned morality might do to women's rights. In
Defending Pornography, the president of the American Civil Liberties
Union takes on those feminists who would have us believe that
censorship equals salvation.

The book, which should be required reading for the customs officers who
regularly infringe on every Canadian's right to read what he or she
pleases, is an effective and thorough debunking of the simplistic idea
that censoring pornography would somehow magically promote a healthier
climate for women. To Strossen, censorship proponents Andrea Dworkin
and Catharine MacKinnon are arch-villains, and the book is an almost
personal attack on them. However, considering that they have both made
it their lives' mission to stamp out freedom of speech, Strossen's
antipathy does not seem out of place.

Strossen shows, through a variety of frightening examples, how
censorship invariable ends up harming those it purports to shield. As
she points out, in Canada it has been gay, lesbian and feminist
writings that have suffered import bans, while good old-fashioned
male-produced smut is as available as ever. 


